A||N||7||BellaFiero||Fuel question||||5||
Z||000000||BellaFiero||07-28-2000||11:30 AM||||First off, I'd like to point out that my fuel gauge does the same thing as many others - it shows a quarter PAST full when full and a quarter tank as empty. Go figure. I always just keep the tank full. But I've noticed that with LESS fuel in the car, my performance suffers. With a full tank, I haul pretty good. Now, I've got the fourbanger - it's an 88. I've got the front Aero nose, the spoiler, the Fi-Air-O over-the-roof twin scoops, a Cobra Jet front hood scoop ( functional,  but mostly just for looks  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> Sorry, but I drive a four-banger - I need as much help in the intimidation factor as I can get ), the exhaust from an 88 GT, and a bunch of mods under the decklid - platinums, wires, etc. and so on, along with a Holley throttle and fuel pump. The fuel pump went in two months ago, so it's still brand-new. I only run Premium in my car, and I've got a 145,500 miles on her ( and she's still running damn strong ). I can ghost most Civics and Del Sols and Camaros and Stangs off the line with a full tank of gas, but with a mostly empty ( at least under half a tank ), it's anybody game. What is up with that?<P>Second question  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I installed the rally gauges this weekend - looks great, got the whole kit and kaboodle for $25.00. Well, it works and looks awesome, but the oil pressure gauge, when I don't put my foot on the gas, reads 0 and the red light comes on. But when I give it gas, it reads and works fine. Now, I'm a shadetree mechanic, I've figured out that the engine is running fine. It has to be something electrical - a sensor or some unit somewhere sending the wrong signal, or going out... anyone had this problem?||||reg||5
Z||000001||moonie||07-28-2000||02:53 PM||||don't know how to fix it , but if its any consolation ....mine does almost the exact same thing.<BR>85 stock 4 cyl <BR>only difference is ...the closer to empty i get , the worse it runs.<BR>at a 1/4 tank , it starts missing <BR>at 1/8 a tank , i'm lucky to get it to roll off at a red light.<BR>i do like you , keep gas in it above 1/2 a tank.<P>moonster||||reg||1
Z||000002||pHoOl||07-29-2000||05:02 PM||||Does premium make the engine run better or faster?  My 88 GT has 130k miles on it, and if you're smoking camaros with four banger, you gotta be doing something good.  Is there a large advantage to the premium fuel?  Cause i always thought if a car was designed to use regular, it wouldn't make a difference.||||reg||1
Z||000003||William Federle||07-29-2000||06:29 PM||||I've filled up my 84 Fiero with the fuel gage half way through the cross-hatched area on the left hand end and it a little over 8 gallons. It runs the same whether it's at<BR>1/4 or on full (but I feel better when it's on full). I have begun using premium full time. I used to use unleaded regular (87) but I noticed that it would ping on hills and when accellerating. I tried filling up with premium one time and 87 the next but that didn't help. My only consolation on that score is that it gets around 33 MPG when the weather's warm.<P>I think I'd try to find some way to hook up a mechanical oil pressure gage to make sure there isn't a real oil pressure problem.<P><p>[This message has been edited by William Federle (edited 07-29-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||BellaFiero||07-29-2000||07:34 PM||||Running on premium is really my only option left. Anything less makes my engine sound like there's a rock bouncing off the valve cover.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> So I bite the extra dime bullet per gallon to have my engine sound good. Now, the car's not all strictly factory here, folks, true, but I didn't mess around much with the fuel lines. And I notice a MASSIVE performance difference with a full tank versus a half or empty tank. Is it that the fuel gets pushed "harder" for lack of a better term? And to clarify a little - I'm talking about your good old '80's stangs and camaros. Your old six cylinders and "occasionaly-maintained" eight cylinders. I've raced Grand Prixs and the Cavalier Z24s and what not and either kept it up with them or killed them. Alot has to do with the driver behind the wheel, but we all know that story. <BR>If I drop it into first with Premium, I can lay some tires down. With regular, I'm lucky if the car MOVES  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <P>------------------<BR>"No, no... you can't put that there, it'll be in the way of the Johnson Rod!"  ||||reg||12
Z||000005||gyro1s||07-31-2000||05:21 AM||||the fuel pump replacement instructions told me to keep fuel levels up or burn out the pump sooner. maybe the pump isn't efficient with low levels? It uses the temp of the fuel to cool itself.(as i understand it) ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Carrolles||07-31-2000||01:38 PM||||Premium gas has a higher Octane rating which means it is harder to ignite.  This allows for running higher compression without preignition (knocking).  Premium has slightly less power per pound than lower octane gas due to the volume taken up by the octane booster.  However the lower BTU content of premium is more than made up for by running a high output engine without pre-ignition.<P>Usually high octane gives no advantage on a stock, clean engine made to run on regular.  ||||reg||1
Z||000007||BellaFiero||07-31-2000||02:31 PM||||I had a plymouth Fury that ran equally well on the lowest end to the highest end of the gasoline spectrum. You couldn't tell what you had put into it, the car ( tank ) trudged along just the same. But for some reason, my Fiero doesn't handle regular as well as Preium. The engine pings and what-not, and my acceleration is very poor - in comparison to the sound and accel of a tank full of premium. I don't mind putting Premium in, you understand. It's just odd to me. I've never heard of a car's performance dependant on the amount of fuel in it's tank… the fuel pump needing high levels to operate at it's peak, and to cool itself - that makes a lot of sense… I guess the answer is simple - fill 'er up.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>||||reg||1
